---
/**
 * Birding for beginners.
 *
 * The biggest gap in Delaware birding information, and the one place this site
 * can create a reader rather than compete for one. An experienced birder
 * already has eBird, a club, a listserv and twenty years of local knowledge.
 * Somebody typing "where can I go see birds" has none of that and is currently
 * served by tourism copy.
 *
 * So: no jargon, no life list, no gear you have to buy before you start.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FeatureIcon from "../../components/FeatureIcon.astro";
import { PRACTICAL } from "../../lib/birding-taxonomy";

const PLACES = [
  { name: "Ashland Nature Center", href: "/indoors/ashland-nature-center/",
    why: "Somebody there knows what you're looking at. The hawk watch runs in autumn and the walks are led." },
  { name: "White Clay Creek State Park", href: "/parks/white-clay-creek/",
    why: "Woodland birds within reach of Newark and Wilmington. No scope, no marsh, no tide to time." },
  { name: "Brandywine Creek State Park", href: "/parks/brandywine-creek/",
    why: "Forest and meadow half an hour from the city. Easy paths and somewhere to park." },
  { name: "Bombay Hook", href: "/birding/bombay-hook/",
    why: "The one on this list you can do from the car, which makes it the most forgiving first refuge — but check the closure dates before driving." },
  { name: "Cape Henlopen State Park", href: "/parks/cape-henlopen/",
    why: "Coast, and the autumn raptor migration. Bring the binoculars you already own." },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Birding for Beginners in Delaware — Where to Start"
  description="New to birding? Five Delaware places to start, the free apps that identify what you see, and an honest answer about what gear you actually need."
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-6">
    <p class="label m-0">Birding</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[18ch] text-marsh">
      Start with the bird you can actually see
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-5 mb-0 text-[1.15rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[54ch]">
      You do not need expensive binoculars, a life list or the ability to tell
      two sparrows apart. You need somewhere to go and about an hour.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4 mb-0 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[54ch]">
      Delaware helps, because a lot of its best birds are big and obvious. A
      great blue heron standing in a marsh does not require skill to enjoy. Nor
      does an osprey, a bald eagle, or several thousand snow geese getting up at
      once in January.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap pb-6">
    <h2 class="label m-0 pb-3">Five places to begin</h2>
    <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 breakout">
      {
        PLACES.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <a href={p.href} class="sign sign-action block no-underline group px-4 py-4 h-full">
              <p class="font-display text-[1.08rem] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] m-0 text-marsh underline decoration-ochre decoration-2 underline-offset-[0.16em] group-hover:decoration-marsh">
                {p.name}
              </p>
              <p class="mt-2 mb-0 text-[0.92rem] leading-snug max-w-[44ch]">{p.why}</p>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <h2>The two free apps worth having</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <a href="https://merlin.allaboutbirds.org/" rel="noopener">Merlin</a> identifies
      a bird from a photo, a description, or by listening to it sing — which is
      the one that will surprise you. <a href="https://ebird.org/" rel="noopener">eBird</a>
      records what you saw and shows what other people are finding nearby. Both
      are free and both are from the Cornell Lab.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Between them they replace the field guide you were about to buy, at least
      until you know whether you want one.
    </p>

    <h2>About binoculars</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Use whatever you have. If you buy, 8× magnification is the usual advice
      for a beginner — a wider field of view makes the bird easier to find, and
      finding it is the hard part. Higher magnification sounds better and shakes
      more.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A scope is a different question and mostly a Bombay Hook question, where
      the birds are across a pool. Nowhere on the list above needs one.
    </p>

    <h2>What the marks on this site mean</h2>
    <ul class="not-prose list-none p-0 m-0 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2 my-4">
      {
        PRACTICAL.map((p) => (
          <li class="flex items-center gap-2 text-[0.95rem]">
            <FeatureIcon icon={p.icon} class="w-[1.15em] h-[1.15em] text-ink" />
            {p.label}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <h2>Two things worth knowing before you go</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Birds are busiest early. Not dawn-heroics early — but a marsh at nine in
      the morning is a different place from a marsh at two in the afternoon.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      And keep your distance. A bird that flies off because you got closer is a
      bird that stopped feeding, and in spring on Delaware Bay that costs it
      more than it costs you. Where a beach or a point is closed for nesting,
      that closure is doing something.
    </p>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/birding/">Where to go birding in Delaware</a></li>
      <li><a href="/birding/birds/">Every bird recorded in Delaware</a></li>
      <li><a href="/good-for/rainy-day/">Somewhere dry, if the weather turns</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
